  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM EDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\n- Low chance of Rain This Weekend\n\nSurface high pressure moves east today and return flow brings \nsome moisture. There could be a few showers this afternoon and\nagain on Sunday as a weak surface boundary moves through today \nfollowed by an area of mid-level frontogenesis on Sunday. For\ntoday, we will follow the hi-res ARW model and have slight chance\nPOPs across the northern zones where the better moisture and lift\nis located. For Sunday, low chance POPs will extend across the\ncentral forecast area.\n\n- Showers and Thunderstorms Possible Late Monday into Tuesday\n\nA shortwave trough ejects out of the northern Rockies with a\nsurface low tracking into Canada early next week. A trailing cold\nfront is expected to touch off showers and thunderstorms Monday\nnight and Tuesday. Instability and deeper moisture is across \nnorthern Lower Michigan pooling ahead of the cold front, which \nsinks south through the forecast area on Tuesday. Precipitable \nwater surges close 2 inches, so heavy rain potential is there as \nthe front will be moving slowly and MBE vectors are less than 10 \nknots.\n\nThe front stalls across the southern zones on Wednesday, with a\nshowers and thunderstorms possible into Thursday as a surface low\ntracks along the front across southern Lower Michigan.",
